<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.7.3: Implementing General Strategies for Factoring</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01701</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.7.3: Implementing General Strategies for Factoring</md:title>
    <md:uuid>26f5bea8-3509-4b7c-9708-3bcaa4fed2e6</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity </title>
<para>Work with a partner on these problems. After each solution, discuss if there are multiple strategies that would work to factor the polynomial.</para>
<para>Find a strategy that fits and factor completely: </para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(5y^3 − 15y^2 − 270y\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>You can use the following video if you need some help.</para>
    <div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
      <div class="os-raise-video-container"><video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
          <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/143008d05c5725e4e42c282ce15ca1124926fe73">
          <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dfa4acbaa03c6f1aa14f4e183fa95bbca397bb0b" srclang="en_us">
        </track></source></video></div>
    </div>
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Factor out GCF of \(5y\). Then use the “undo FOIL” method on the polynomial of the form \(x^2 + bx +c\).
      \(5y(y − 9)(y + 6)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(75m^3 + 12m\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Factor out GCF of \(3m\).
      \(3m(25m^2 + 4)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(16x^2 + 24xy − 4x − 6y\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Factor out GCF of \(2\). Then use the grouping method.
      \(2(4x − 1)(2x + 3y)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(2n^2 + 13n − 7\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Use the “\(ac\)” method or trial and error.
      \((2n − 1)(n + 7)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(121r^2 − s^2\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Use the difference of squares method.
      \((11r − s)(11r + s)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(25w^2 − 60w + 36\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Use the perfect square trinomial method.
      \((5w − 6)^2\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \((3x + 1)^2 − 6(3x + 1) + 9\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Use the substitution or perfect square trinomial method.
      \((3x−2)^2\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(16x^2 − 24xy + 9y^2 − 64\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Use the grouping method on the first three terms. They are a perfect square trinomial. Then use the difference of squares on the result.
      \((4x − 3y − 8)(4x − 3y + 8)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<title>Video: Determining Factoring Method</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about how to determine which method of factoring will be best to apply.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <div class="os-raise-video-container"><video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
